# EXPECT: assigns enclosing name
#
# A `local` declared CONDITIONALLY inside a nested define, followed by a bare
# write to the same name.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is the restriction on its sibling. t111 lets a
# top-level `local` in a nested define count as the define's own name, so it is
# no longer refused as outward mutation. That relaxation is only sound at the
# TOP level, because `local` is not block-scoped inside a function body:
#
#   define b(d) as: if d == 1: local x is 7 / x is 5
#   b of 1  ->  the declaration ran; the outer x is untouched
#   b of 0  ->  the declaration did NOT run; `x is 5` writes the OUTER x
#
# Measured on the VM, not assumed — `f of 0` prints 5, the clobbered value.
# So a conditionally-declared name is genuine outward mutation on the path where
# the declaration is skipped, and must keep refusing.
#
# Without this fixture the obvious generalisation of t111's fix — seed every
# `local` name in the body — passes every gate and silently reintroduces the
# outward write the guard exists to stop.

define f(y) as:
    local x is 100
    define b(d) as:
        if d == 1:
            local x is 7
        x is 5
        return d
    local t is b of y
    return x

print of (f of 0)
